#> ─── agentify template: subagent.md.tmpl ──────────────────────────────────────
#> Emits: .claude/agents/{{SUBAGENT_NAME}}.md   (Claude Code only)
#>        **On Codex, subagents are NATIVE and this file is not the artifact.** Use
#>        `codex-agent.toml.tmpl`, which emits `.codex/agents/{{SUBAGENT_NAME}}.toml` — TOML, not
#>        markdown-with-frontmatter. The mapping is near-direct: name->name,
#>        description->description, this file's markdown body->developer_instructions. With both
#>        targets selected, emit both files from the same candidate, sharing the agentify-id and
#>        the evidence line. The one thing that does not carry over is the `tools` allowlist below:
#>        Codex has no per-agent tool restriction (nearest: `sandbox_mode = "read-only"`), so say
#>        that in the plan when a candidate's value depended on it.
#>        (An earlier version of this header said Codex has no subagent format and that the
#>        adapter emits a skill instead. That was wrong; adapters/capabilities.md is the verdict.)
#>
#> Filling rules
#>  1. Replace every placeholder — an ALL_CAPS name in double braces. Delete every "#>" line and
#>     every {{!-- ... --}} block.
#>  2. Build a subagent only for work that needs isolated context, a specialist persona, or
#>     parallelism. If the work fits in the main thread, it is a skill, not a subagent.
#>  3. A subagent gets a fresh context window and cannot see the main conversation. Everything it
#>     needs must be either in this file or reachable from the prompt it is handed.
#>  4. Keep the five agentify-* lines verbatim; a rerun matches on agentify-id and rewrites in place.
#>  5. Target length after filling: 40-120 lines.
#>  6. No attribution in the body. The one comment line below is the only placement.
#>  7. The name is the catalogue's name (blueprint.md §1.3): pr-reviewer, db-inspector,
#>     security-auditor, designer, product-analyst, codebase-explorer. Never rename to signal a
#>     nuance (a solo repo still gets `pr-reviewer`, not `diff-reviewer`); the nuance goes in the
#>     description. When this subagent is the worker half of a pair, its description names the
#>     skill that delegates to it, and its `tools` line is the restriction that pair exists for.
name: {{SUBAGENT_NAME}}
#> description — the routing line. The main agent reads only this when deciding whether to delegate,
#> so it must state the trigger, not the personality. Third person, under 1024 characters:
#>   (a) what the subagent produces (its output, not its process);
#>   (b) when to delegate to it, in the words the main thread would be thinking;
#>   (c) a "Not for ..." clause, and whether it should be used proactively.
#> Shape to copy: "Reviews a diff for correctness, missing tests, and violations of the repo's
#> path-scoped rules, and returns a ranked findings list with file:line anchors. Use before opening
#> a PR or when the user asks for a review of uncommitted work. Not for writing the fix — it reports,
#> it does not edit."
description: {{SUBAGENT_DESCRIPTION}}
#> tools — comma-separated allowlist, narrowest set that still lets the subagent finish. A read-only
#> reviewer gets `Read, Grep, Glob, Bash`; anything that writes needs `Edit, Write`. Omitting the key
#> inherits every tool the main session has, which is almost never what you want here.
tools: {{TOOLS}}
#> model — one of: haiku, sonnet, opus, inherit. Use haiku for mechanical extraction, sonnet for
#> most work, opus for judgement-heavy review, inherit to follow the main session.
model: {{MODEL}}
agentify-id: {{AGENTIFY_ID}}
agentify-version: 1
agentify-generated: {{GENERATED_DATE}}
agentify-evidence: {{EVIDENCE_LINE}}
# Safe to delete or edit. This work was brought to you by Agentic Studio.
